7] [17592186087688 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "43401830-730c-57d7-8831-9a0580042ffe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1045 "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087910 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "16a4de81-848b-5638-b998-ac9062204da4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088003 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $8x - y = 2$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18660893-6110-5df8-bfd8-f3d922709ebc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088068 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $8x - 2y =-2$ and $kx - 5y = 15$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93f5de54-6bcd-5688-b2b5-e060b8822757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088504 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088504 #edb/ref 1061 144181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088504 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cab8d9d2-3173-5553-86f8-e9fb1aecfd16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088504 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088505 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088505 #edb/ref 1061 144182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088505 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "212176f8-846a-5cf2-ae65-698c2a23cb29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088505 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088506 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088506 #edb/ref 1061 144186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088506 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c4f6496-c80d-521c-a439-5e19df77d71b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088506 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088507 #edb/ref 1061 144190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088507 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67b4f73a-8039-56d5-a90d-46d17f45eab1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088507 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088989 #edb/ref 1064 "Two lines that never cross are said to be **parallel**. For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nFor example, the lines\n\n$$\ny=2x+4\n$$\n\nand $y=2x-5$ have the same slope of $m=2$, so they are parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines never cross.\n\n![](images/c7/c7d36372d9646d0ce10eff5d35a4f9b8e56a7b37b333c5d53ac243065ee751af.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the lines\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+4\n$$\n\nand $y=2x-5$ have different slopes, so they are *not* parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines *do* cross.\n\n![](images/92/92c1ee39ccbbbf20dc3f4a7f76a1114d975fb0826eeac13d273b4b4fd9a83105.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088989 #edb/ref 1065 8902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088989 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2a7600a0-ce9a-5126-866d-bc3d1cf82b41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088989 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477089 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{2}{5}x + 2$ and $y = mx + 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4976d972-daca-5ee7-9f71-69b46357d139" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477079 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x+2$ is $\\frac{2}{5}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+9$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{2}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477082 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{12}{5}x - 3$ and $y = mx - 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab1aa788-0fc2-5c61-9a77-7c9bf36f472b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477083 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{12}{5}x-3$ is $\\frac{12}{5}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx-9$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{12}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477081 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{7}{9}x + 8$ and $y = mx - 2$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af16e858-af23-50a8-8659-81ccfff82acf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477084 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{7}{9}x+8$ is $\\frac{7}{9}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx-2$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{7}{9}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477076 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{3}{8}x + 3$ and $y = mx + 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4217e45-b7c2-59c9-8002-88e6fed81360" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477087 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{3}{8}x+3$ is $\\frac{3}{8}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+9$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{3}{8}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477075 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = \\frac{5}{9}x - 3$ and $y = mx + 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $m$?\n\n$m =$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d68e52d7-e69b-5cfa-b2c4-2924c200434d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477088 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{5}{9}x-3$ is $\\frac{5}{9}$.\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+12$ is $m$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{5}{9}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477090 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186087686.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477090 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "0c60c388-88bc-57f0-8030-730e499275e6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478521 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $-21x - 12y = 108$ and $kx + 3y = 9$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04208a31-b2ae-567f-8896-e86342d6ce9d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478499 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $-21x - 12y = 108$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n-21x - 12y = 108\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -12y = 21x + 108\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = - \\frac{7}{4}x - 9.\n$$\nIts slope is $- \\frac{7}{4}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 3y = 9$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 3y = 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 3y = -kx + 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{3}x + 3.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{3}=- \\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-3$ gives $k=\\frac{21}{4}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478510 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $24x - 10y = 70$ and $kx + 4y = 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "211d09f0-9544-5c60-a27a-1e3939da787d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478500 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $24x - 10y = 70$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n24x - 10y = 70\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -10y = -24x + 70\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{12}{5}x - 7.\n$$\nIts slope is $\\frac{12}{5}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 4y = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 4y = 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 4y = -kx + 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{4}x + 3.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{4}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{4}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-4$ gives $k=- \\frac{48}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478522 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = -7x + 0$ and $kx + 9y = -5$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b6bd912-687a-541d-a6c5-b5a879018279" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478503 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = -7x + 0$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-7$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx + 9y = -5$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx + 9y = -5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 9y = -kx - 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac{k}{9}x - \\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\dfrac{k}{9}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{k}{9}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $-9$ gives $k=63$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478512 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $x - 4y = 48$ and $kx - 4y = 12$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dafc1fb-015c-56ff-bc14-52effb959f64" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478508 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $x - 4y = 48$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nx - 4y = 48\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -4y = -x + 48\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{1}{4}x - 12.\n$$\nIts slope is $\\frac{1}{4}$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 4y = 12$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 4y = 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -4y = -kx + 12\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{4}x - 3.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{4}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$ gives $k=1$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478505 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1071 "The lines $y = 3x + 6$ and $kx - 6y = -9$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72dbd957-6f59-540e-9ca7-2b36673b5f34" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186478514 #edb/ref 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y = 3x + 6$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $3$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx - 6y = -9$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\nkx - 6y = -9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -6y = -kx - 9\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\frac{k}{6}x + \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{6}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{6}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $6$ giv